WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.000
Run an owned ISSM clone with a changed thickness profile.
Why: Keep the source inputs, inspect native fields, and save a complete result package.
Look for: Prepare Square.par with only hmax 1000→1010 m; keep hmin = 300 m.

2
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Start here in CryoLauncher. Choose Cloud for this tutorial.
Why: Your workspace keeps scripts, dataset references and results together.
Look for: Your workspace keeps scripts, datasets, run history and results together.

3
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:34.000
Choose Cloud → Advanced → ISSM → SquareIceShelf.
Why: Choose the intended interface, model and example before editing or launching a run.
Look for: Retain your configured MATLAB license and required Connector.

4
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:43.000
Enter a new name and choose Clone to My Workspace.
Why: Cloning gives you an editable copy while preserving the supplied application source.
Look for: The canonical example remains read-only.

5
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:55.000
Wait for the cloned-workspace confirmation.
Why: Cloning gives you an editable copy while preserving the supplied application source.
Look for: Keep the native example files in your owned clone.

6
00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Choose the changed Square.par in your file picker; Open.
Why: Selecting local files prepares them for import; importing saves them in this example.
Look for: Keep runme.m, DomainOutline.exp and Front.exp unchanged.

7
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:15.000
Check that one script file is selected.
Why: Selecting local files prepares them for import; importing saves them in this example.
Look for: You are replacing Square.par in the owned clone.

8
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:24.000
Enable Replace existing script files.
Why: Replacement explicitly allows an imported file to overwrite the same name in this clone.
Look for: The clone already contains Square.par.

9
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:34.000
Choose Import script files.
Why: Import saves the selected source files in this example; it does not launch the model.
Look for: Import the intended hmax = 1010 m parameter file.

10
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:46.000
Check Imported Square.par. Files are saved.
Why: Import saves the selected source files in this example; it does not launch the model.
Look for: The submitted parameter file was checked against the intended copy.

11
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:58.000
Keep runme.m as the Run target.
Why: Run target selects the entry script; other imported files can act as helpers.
Look for: The +10 m change at hmax tapers along y; it is not a uniform addition.

12
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Choose Review & Launch.
Why: Review confirms the intended model, source, resources and charges before submission.
Look for: Retain the existing licensed MATLAB workflow.

13
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Confirm ISSM and pilot-issm-advanced-1119.
Why: Review confirms the intended model, source, resources and charges before submission.
Look for: The native job used 2 vCPU and 8 GiB.

14
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:25.000
Control guide: confirm Launch cloud run when checks are Ready.
Why: This confirmation stages the reviewed inputs and starts a paid cloud job.
Look for: The following card belongs to this tutorial’s original native run.

15
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Wait for the new ISSM Advanced run card.
Why: The previous card remains visible while the new run is registered.
Look for: ISSM · pilot-issm-advanced-1119, then Submitting.

16
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:43.000
Select StressbalanceSolution → Vel; Render when needed.
Why: The solution, field name, units and mesh counts identify what you are viewing.
Look for: The first field may preview automatically.

17
00:02:43.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Read the native field summary.
Why: The solution, field name, units and mesh counts identify what you are viewing.
Look for: Vx, Vy, Vel and Pressure are finite at all 91 nodes; 148 triangles.

18
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:12.000
Open the full unchanged saved velocity PNG.
Why: The unchanged saved figure preserves the complete map, axes and colour scale.
Look for: Speed: 0–9272.778005 m/year; x and y are metres.

19
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:24.000
Read convergence as a diagnostic, rather than transient time.
Why: A steady diagnostic convergence count is different from a transient time axis.
Look for: 23 steps is a scalar stress-balance convergence count.

20
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:36.000
Keep the native completion and runtime provenance.
Why: Submission, queue/startup and native model execution are separate stages.
Look for: Submission → start: 183.642 s; Batch task: 36.831 s.

21
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:44.000
Choose Download results.
Why: The archive keeps this run’s numeric outputs, figures and provenance together.
Look for: Wait for this run’s fresh HTTPS link.

22
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:54.000
Open the new link and save the 11-member ZIP.
Why: The archive keeps this run’s numeric outputs, figures and provenance together.
Look for: Keep it with your modified Square.par and native source files.

23
00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:08.000
Retain the changed-input context with your result.
Why: Keep the scientific input definitions and numerical qualifications with the result.
